 ## Overview
-Hierarchical Partition Optimization (HPO) is an offline preprocessing method that computes an optimal adaptive partitioning of a volume dataset and assigns extraction methods to each partition block to minimize a combined cost of memory usage and extraction time [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of balancing memory constraints against rendering performance by constructing a hierarchical tree of partitions where each node is either a single block or a subdivision into children, optimized via a Lagrangian relaxation [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Min/Max Tree Construction](1623-min-max-tree-construction.md) by explicitly optimizing for a trade-off between two competing metrics (space and time) rather than just geometric homogeneity or structural compactness, and from [Octree Encoding](5851-octree-encoding.md) by focusing on runtime resource allocation rather than storage bit-packing.
